WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.689 --> 00:00:03.250
In Hollywood, films are judged by the bottom
line.

00:00:03.250 --> 00:00:06.890
How much money did they make, how much money
did they cost, and how much cash did the studio

00:00:06.890 --> 00:00:08.189
executive get to pocket?

00:00:08.189 --> 00:00:12.720
But sometimes, movies that are flops at the
box office get an unexpected second life,

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:15.480
becoming cult classics as audiences slowly
discover them.

00:00:15.490 --> 00:00:20.140
Here's a look at some beloved movies that
totally bombed at the box office.

00:00:20.150 --> 00:00:21.210
Highlander

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:25.350
While today it's considered one of the best
sword-and-sorcery fantasy films of the '80s,

00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:31.670
Highlander wasn't well-received when it premiered
in 1986, earning a grand total of $12.9 million

00:00:31.670 --> 00:00:34.350
against a $16 million production cost

00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:38.449
Once Highlander was released for the video-rental
market, though, it did much better with viewers

00:00:38.449 --> 00:00:39.850
and gained a cult following.

00:00:39.850 --> 00:00:43.909
The fanbase grew so large that high demand
eventually resulted in the creation of five

00:00:43.909 --> 00:00:48.249
sequel films, a live-action television series
that ran for six seasons, a series of books,

00:00:48.249 --> 00:00:51.749
and even comics, with a big screen reboot
rumored to be in the works.

00:00:51.749 --> 00:00:54.999
So much for the claim there could be only
one!

00:00:54.999 --> 00:00:56.579
The Boondock Saints

00:00:56.579 --> 00:01:01.030
If it hadn't been for now-defunct video rental
chain Blockbuster, The Boondock Saints never

00:01:01.030 --> 00:01:02.460
would have stood a chance.

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:08.000
Showing in only five theaters in the U.S.,
the film earned just $411,000 worldwide in

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:10.480
1999 despite costing $6 million to make.

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:15.350
Then Blockbuster stepped in, inking a deal
to distribute the film as a "Blockbuster Exclusive."

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:20.230
The movie soon gained a massive cult following,
bringing in over $50 million in DVD/Blu-ray

00:01:20.230 --> 00:01:25.580
sales, spawning a sequel in 2009 and a new
TV series - one without stars Norman Reedus

00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:27.410
and Sean Patrick Flannery.

00:01:27.410 --> 00:01:29.230
Scott PIlgrim vs. The World

00:01:29.230 --> 00:01:33.590
Critics loved Scott Pilgrim vs. the World,
calling it "funny," "heartfelt," "hilarious,"

00:01:33.590 --> 00:01:35.560
"vivid," and "wildly inventive."

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:39.180
Unfortunately, though, critics seemed to be
the only people who actually went to the theater

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:43.390
to see it, as Scott Pilgrim failed to earn
back its production costs, earning less than

00:01:43.390 --> 00:01:48.250
$48 million worldwide against a reported $60
million production budget.

00:01:48.250 --> 00:01:53.640
Throw in another $25 million spent on advertising,
and Scott Pilgrim seemed doomed to be a massive

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:58.350
bomb - until it sold over 300,000 copies on
Blu-ray and DVD in its first week alone.

00:01:58.350 --> 00:01:59.659
Talk about your instant cult classic!

00:01:59.659 --> 00:02:00.659
Dredd

00:02:00.659 --> 00:02:05.030
Despite a built in fan base from the comics,
and widespread critical acclaim, the 2012

00:02:05.030 --> 00:02:10.170
action flick Dredd earned just $41 million
worldwide, which sounds like a lot until you

00:02:10.170 --> 00:02:14.940
consider it cost $50 million to make and another
$25 million to market.

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:19.240
Part of that might be lingering bad taste
from the 1995 Sylvester Stallone version,

00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:23.260
Judge Dredd, but much of it had to do with
bad timing: just a year earlier, the cult

00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:27.641
hit The Raid: Redemption featured almost the
exact same plot, leading many fans to consider

00:02:27.641 --> 00:02:29.920
Dredd a second rate copy.

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:32.200
"Judgment time!"

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:35.810
Whatever the reasons for its theatrical failure,
nothing could keep Dredd down once it hit

00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:38.200
the home market, where it became an instant
success.

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:44.360
Dredd sold 650,000 copies in the first week
it was available on DVD and Blu-ray, and by

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:48.910
2017, its total video sales earnings topped
the $20 million mark, leading to rumors of

00:02:48.910 --> 00:02:50.610
a possible revival of the franchise.

00:02:50.610 --> 00:02:53.090
"I am the law."

00:02:53.090 --> 00:02:54.700
The Chronicles of Riddick

00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:58.790
After first appearing in the 2000 film Pitch
Black, Vin Diesel reprised the role of Richard

00:02:58.790 --> 00:03:01.950
B. Riddick in the 2004 sci-fi epic The Chronicles
of Riddick.

00:03:01.950 --> 00:03:06.430
With a budget of $120 million, it needed to
be a hit just to break even.

00:03:06.430 --> 00:03:10.990
It wasn't, and it didn't, earning just $115
million worldwide.

00:03:10.990 --> 00:03:14.770
Diesel was even nominated for a Razzie Award
for Worst Actor for his effort.

00:03:14.770 --> 00:03:18.849
Eventually, though, fans caught on and slowly
turned The Chronicles of Riddick into a cult

00:03:18.849 --> 00:03:23.569
hit, eventually spawning a 2013 sequel, as
well as numerous books, action figures, and

00:03:23.569 --> 00:03:24.840
even video games.

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:27.560
"I bow to no man."

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:29.700
The Rocky Horror Picture Show

00:03:29.709 --> 00:03:33.430
It may be the movie with the longest-ever
theatrical run, but The Rocky Horror Picture

00:03:33.430 --> 00:03:36.099
Show bombed during its initial release in
1975.

00:03:36.099 --> 00:03:43.230
Although it was based on a popular stage musical,
the film adaptation earned only $21,245 in

00:03:43.230 --> 00:03:44.370
its opening weekend.

00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:49.020
The studio yanked the film from most locations,
but one exec had a brilliant idea: why not

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:52.540
run it as one of the midnight shows in the
Waverly Theatre in New York City?

00:03:52.540 --> 00:03:56.680
Literally overnight, it became a hit with
the Waverly crowd, who just a few months later

00:03:56.680 --> 00:04:00.660
turned it into an interactive experience by
shouting improvised lines at the screen and

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:01.660
singing along.

00:04:01.660 --> 00:04:04.810
The midnight showings soon spread to other
theaters around the country, along with the

00:04:04.810 --> 00:04:06.920
habit of shouted audience "call-backs."

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:11.400
Eventually, dedicated fans began to form "shadow-cast"
performance troupes—acting out the film

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:14.690
in costume on stage as the movie plays out
on the big screen behind them.

00:04:14.690 --> 00:04:19.310
Within a year, Rocky Horror went from a box-office
bomb to a cult sensation that's still going

00:04:19.310 --> 00:04:21.320
strong over 40 years later.

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:24.580
"I see you shiver, with antici ---"

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:27.870
"--- pation!"

